# Build output.
/target/

# Environment. `.env.example` is committed and `.env` never is: a real one holds
# the database password, and `NOCTUA.md §15.5` puts secrets in ephemeral
# environment variables at the process boundary rather than in a file a clone
# could carry. A gate in `cargo xtask check` fails if one appears anyway.
#
# Not anchored to the root: a service directory will one day have its own, and an
# anchored pattern would ignore neither it nor catch it.
# Every shape of it, not the one spelling, and `env` is a dot-separated part of
# the name rather than its prefix. `.env.local`, `env.production`,
# `secrets.env.local` and `production.env` are all conventional names for a file
# holding real credentials; a prefix rule ignores the first and lets the other
# three through, so three of the four would go into a clone.
#
# The template is un-ignored by what makes it a template — any part saying so,
# not the single spelling `.env.example`, which would leave `.env.local.example`  # allow-reference: names `.env.local.example`, the shape this rule un-ignores, not a file here
# ignored and therefore missing from a clone that needs it. A part is a part
# wherever it sits, so each keyword is un-ignored last, first and in the middle:
# `example.env` is as much a template as `.env.example`, and a rule that only
# knows the last position hides it.
.env
.env*
*.env
*.env.*
env
env.*

!*.example
!*.sample
!*.template
!*.dist
!example.*
!sample.*
!template.*
!dist.*
!*.example.*
!*.sample.*
!*.template.*
!*.dist.*

# A file with a type is that type. `env.rs` is a Rust module and `env.toml` a  # allow-reference: names `env.rs` and `env.toml`, the shapes this rule is about, not files here
# manifest; `env.*` above hides both, so without the negations below a clone
# misses two source files. The
# formats this tree holds, which is the same list `holds_an_environment` in
# `xtask/src/gates.rs` reads — the two files decide the same names, and a test
# there fails if they ever stop agreeing.
#
# **A negation by format, `!*.rs` rather than `!env.rs`, is too wide, and `!*.sh` is the dangerous one.**  # allow-reference: names `!env.rs`, the negation this paragraph is about, not a file here
# `sh`, `json`, `yaml` and `toml` are the formats an environment is written in,
# so `!*.sh` un-ignores `.env.sh` — `source .env.sh` is how such a file is used —
# and a real one goes into a clone. Measured, with `!*.sh`: `.env.sh` holding a
# database URL and an AWS secret, `.env.json` holding a password, and  # allow-reference: names `.env.json`, a file a measurement wrote to prove the hole, not one here
# `ok no .env is committed, in 165 path(s)` with `check passed`. The negations
# below name the one file each rule above over-matches, which is `env.<format>`
# and nothing else.
#
# **And the same five formats are not un-ignored here either**, the same mistake
# in the other direction: a list naming every format tracks `env.toml`.  # allow-reference: names `env.toml`, the shape the rule above over-matches, not a file here
# Measured, one such file under `policy/` holding `DATABASE_URL` and
# `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` — `check passed` online and `--offline` both, the
# whole run differing from a clean one by the path count going up by one. A file
# named `env` wearing a config or shell format is an environment; a module named
# `env` wears one of the kinds left below.
!env.rs
!env.md
!env.sql
!env.lock
!env.cedar
!env.cedarschema
!env.tsv

# **Every rule below the negations above, not above them.** Git takes the last
# pattern that matches, so a new ignore rule written higher up would be undone by
# `!*.md` or `!*.rs` for any file whose name ends that way — silently, and for
# exactly the private files a rule like that is usually added to hide.
#
# The developer's private, machine-specific agent notes. See `NOCTUA.md §3.4`.
AGENTS.local.md

# OS cruft.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# **Not committed, and refused by the gate if it is.** `docker compose` merges
# this file without being asked, so it is a second definition of the stack that
# every gate about `docker-compose.yml` is blind to. Ignored here so a local one
# is not committed by accident, and `cargo xtask check` refuses one that is.
docker-compose.override.yml

# `xtask/mutants.py` leaves one beside itself. It is not readable as text, so
# the marker census counts it among the files it walked and cannot read one —
# `the marker census read 167 file(s) of 168` — which is a real failure about a
# file nobody meant to create.
__pycache__/

# The same runner's sentinel, which exists only while a source on disk is not
# the source that run was handed. It is deliberately *not* cleaned up on a kill:
# finding it is how the next run knows to refuse rather than take a weakening as
# its baseline. Measured — a `SIGKILL` can leave `xtask/src/gates.rs` holding
# `.any(|(named, found)| found == digest)`, the repository half of a digest pin
# simply gone, and without the sentinel the next run would write that back as
# the restored tree.
#
# **It lives under `target/`**, because a file with no extension under a walked
# tree fails `no_file_of_an_unknown_kind_sits_in_this_repository` —
# `xtask/mutants.py` says so in as many words. The blanket `target/` rule above
# covers it already; it is named here so that this file and the runner name the
# same path.
target/.mutants-in-flight
